(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is a patient with cardiomyopathy referred to cardiology?","choices":["Only once symptoms occur at rest","Only when a device must be implanted","Only after an admission for edema","To establish care and review risk features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A patient with cardiomyopathy is referred to cardiology to establish care, consider genetic testing, review any high-risk features, and discuss medications or the need for an intervention. This applies across the three types: dilated, hypertrophic and restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which group is particularly susceptible to acute myocarditis?","choices":["Smokers","Young males","Older women","Infants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute myocarditis has no race predilection and incidence is similar between males and females, but young males are particularly susceptible. Most patients with mild symptoms recover completely, while one third later develop dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How often is heart muscle biopsy useful in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Rarely; not used routinely","Always, to confirm it","Routinely, before drugs","Yearly, for monitoring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endocardial biopsy is not used routinely in dilated cardiomyopathy and is rarely useful. It is reserved for recent onset of rapidly deteriorating cardiac function or a history of systemic disease with possible cardiac involvement, such as hemochromatosis, sarcoidosis or amyloidosis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which device therapies are part of adult cardiomyopathy care?","choices":["Carotid stent or vena cava filter devices","Temporary external pacing, as needed","Defibrillator or biventricular pacemaker","Intra-aortic balloon pump for all patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Device therapy for adults with cardiomyopathy is an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) or a biventricular pacemaker, alongside guideline-directed drugs and lifestyle changes. A left ventricular assist device (LVAD) or transplant is considered in advanced cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which laboratory results are typical of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["High lipase and amylase","Raised troponin and peptide levels","Normal troponin, normal peptide","High ferritin and transferrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, troponin I and T are usually elevated and B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) is frequently elevated. With ST elevation and wall-motion abnormalities, these results make it mimic acute coronary syndrome until catheterization shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"When is cardiac catheterization obtained in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Yearly, for surveillance","Before any beta blocker","Before a pacemaker or surgery","To make the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization is not required for diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; it is obtained when invasive therapy, a pacemaker or surgery, is planned. Noninvasive tests carry the rest: cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, exercise echo and 24 to 48 hours of ambulatory electrocardiogram monitoring. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Why may a permanent pacemaker be needed in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Outflow obstruction on exertion","A low ejection fraction on echo","Recurrent atrial thrombus","Nodal fibrosis causing heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, fibrosis of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes can lead to heart block, so a permanent pacemaker is placed for conduction system disease. Anticoagulants for atrial thrombi and, finally, cardiac transplantation are the other later steps. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which heart sound is often present in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["S4, from a noncompliant ventricle","S3, from a compliant ventricle","An opening snap after S2","An ejection click before S2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fourth heart sound (S4) is often present in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy because the left ventricle is noncompliant. The rest of the examination fits: blood pressure and heart rate usually normal, pulsus bisferiens, and a forceful, sustained apical impulse. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the diastolic abnormality of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without obstruction?","choices":["Reduced compliance, high filling pressure","Increased compliance, low filling pressure","Weak contraction and a huge cavity","Pericardial fluid limiting filling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without obstruction, the hypertrophied myocardium resists diastolic ventricular filling (reduced compliance). The left ventricular end-diastolic pressure is elevated, a reflection of that decreased compliance. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Besides valve regurgitation, which complications does dilated cardiomyopathy put patients at risk for?","choices":["Arrhythmias and thromboembolism","Outflow obstruction and syncope","Heart block from nodal fibrosis","Plaque rupture and infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In dilated cardiomyopathy, mitral and tricuspid regurgitation develop as the valve apparatus distorts, end-systolic volumes rise and drive further dilation, and patients are at risk for arrhythmias and thromboembolic complications. The impaired contraction leads to systolic dysfunction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which side of heart failure often dominates in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Left-sided failure","Neither side at all","High-output failure","Right-sided failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Even though filling pressures are elevated in both ventricles in restrictive cardiomyopathy, right heart failure may dominate: abdominal pain from hepatomegaly, bilateral pedal edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Patients may be more comfortable sitting for the examination. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Why is a high degree of suspicion needed to identify acute myocarditis?","choices":["It mainly affects older adults","Biopsy cannot confirm the diagnosis","Many cases are not clinically obvious","Troponin stays normal throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Many cases of myocarditis are not clinically obvious, with presentations ranging from subtle to devastating, so a high degree of suspicion is required to identify acute myocarditis. It usually arises in an otherwise healthy person and can progress rapidly to heart failure and arrhythmia. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 47, 50, 54, 56"},{"q":"What proportion of patients with mild myocarditis later develop dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Nearly all","None","One third","One half"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with mild myocarditis symptoms recover completely, but one third subsequently develop dilated cardiomyopathy. Young males are particularly susceptible to myocarditis, and there is no race predilection. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which region does the hypertrophy of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy most frequently involve?","choices":["Right ventricular wall","Left atrial wall","Ventricular apex only","Interventricular septum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hypertrophy of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is often asymmetric and can involve any region of the left ventricle, but it most frequently involves the interventricular septum. It occurs without a causative hemodynamic factor such as hypertension or aortic valve disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the most common symptoms of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Claudication and pallor","Fever and myalgia first","Ascites and pedal edema","Chest pain and dyspnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest pain and dyspnea are the most common symptoms of Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, with palpitations, nausea, vomiting and syncope also possible. Because it mimics acute coronary syndrome, the examination may look like acute coronary syndrome or acute heart failure. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Why must diuretics be used cautiously in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["They worsen iron overload","They thicken the walls","Stiff ventricles need preload","They cause heart block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics treat pulmonary and systemic congestion in restrictive cardiomyopathy, but they lower preload, and noncompliant ventricles depend on preload to maintain cardiac output. Too much diuresis can therefore drop output in a disease that already has the poorest prognosis of all heart muscle diseases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which serious outcome can the arrhythmias of acute myocarditis cause?","choices":["Septal hypertrophy","Pericardial effusion","Sudden cardiac death","Aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In acute myocarditis, underlying ventricular arrhythmias or atrioventricular block can cause palpitations, syncope or sudden cardiac death. The heart failure picture may include tachycardia, hypotension, raised jugular venous pressure, a third heart sound gallop, crackles and peripheral edema. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which infection from beta-hemolytic streptococcus involves the heart in 50% to 90% of cases?","choices":["Q fever","Rheumatic fever","Lyme disease","Diphtheria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatic fever, caused by beta-hemolytic streptococcus, affects the heart in 50% to 90% of cases and is among the infectious causes of myocarditis. The other bacterial causes are listed separately: diphtheria, brucellosis, clostridial infections, legionnaires disease, meningococcal infection and Mycoplasma pneumonia. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which activity advice has classically been given to patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Competitive sport if asymptomatic","Complete bed rest indefinitely","High-intensity interval training","Mild to moderate, noncompetitive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Historically, patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy have been told to confine themselves to mild to moderate recreational activity in a noncompetitive manner. They are also advised to maintain hydration and avoid excess alcohol. Sudden cardiac death is the leading cause of mortality in this disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram pattern fits restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Big atria, near-normal ventricles","Dilated, thin-walled ventricles","Small cavity, septal bulge","Apex akinetic, base normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy shows marked enlargement of one or both atria, normal or near normal left ventricular systolic function and cavity size, normal or thickened ventricular walls, preserved ejection fraction and abnormal diastolic function. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging helps separate it from constrictive pericarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which drug is the aldosterone receptor blocker used to manage dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Amiodarone","Spironolactone","Metoprolol","Verapamil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is the aldosterone receptor blocker on the drug list for dilated cardiomyopathy, alongside angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta blockers, angiotensin receptor blockers, sacubitril/valsartan, nitrates, diuretics, digoxin as a second-line drug, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulants. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 42, 76, 78"},{"q":"Which drugs may benefit restrictive cardiomyopathy by lengthening left ventricular filling time?","choices":["Antibiotics, steroids or antivirals","Beta blockers, verapamil or diltiazem","Digoxin, epinephrine or norepinephrine","Nifedipine, nitrates or hydralazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, beta blockers and cardioselective calcium channel blockers such as verapamil or diltiazem may help by increasing left ventricular filling time, improving ventricular relaxation and decreasing compensatory sympathetic stimulation. They are negative chronotropes and negative inotropes. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 77, 98"},{"q":"What characterizes restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Transient apical ballooning after stress","Impaired filling, near-normal chamber size","Dilated chambers with thin, weak walls","Asymmetric septal thickening of 1.5 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive cardiomyopathy, the least common cardiomyopathy, is characterized by impaired diastolic filling with reasonably preserved left ventricular chamber size and wall thickness that is normal or slightly increased. Ejection fraction stays preserved until late stages, both atria enlarge, and right heart failure is often prominent. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which medication is a recognized cause of myocarditis?","choices":["Amiodarone","Methyldopa","Metoprolol","Lisinopril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methyldopa is among the medications that cause myocarditis, along with penicillin, thiazide diuretics and tetracycline. Toxic exposures (lithium, arsenic, lead, copper) and autoimmune diseases (systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis) are the other noninfectious causes. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which parasite causes dilated cardiomyopathy through Chagas disease?","choices":["Toxoplasma gondii","Plasmodium falciparum","Giardia lamblia","Trypanosoma cruzi"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Trypanosoma cruzi causes Chagas disease, the parasitic infectious cause of dilated cardiomyopathy listed beside viral causes such as coxsackievirus, parvovirus B19, influenza and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). It also appears among the parasitic causes of acute myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 22, 48"},{"q":"How should suspected Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy be evaluated and treated at first?","choices":["As acute coronary syndrome","As a panic attack at home","As viral myocarditis first","As restrictive disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences: troponin is usually elevated, the electrocardiogram shows ST elevation and T wave inversion, and echo shows wall-motion abnormalities. So evaluation and treatment follow established acute coronary syndrome protocols, with admission to cardiology. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What do Kerley B lines on a chest radiograph indicate?","choices":["Pulmonary edema","Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Lung fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are 1 to 2 cm long horizontal lines that meet the pleura at right angles, and they indicate pulmonary edema. In dilated cardiomyopathy the same chest radiograph may also show cardiomegaly, pleural effusions and frank pulmonary edema. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which self-care point is advised in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Stay well hydrated","Limit fluids strictly","Drink more alcohol","Use nitrates freely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are advised to maintain hydration and avoid dehydration, along with avoiding excess alcohol and keeping to mild to moderate, noncompetitive recreational activity. Sudden cardiac death remains the leading cause of mortality in this disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram finding confirms a clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Apical ballooning with a normal base","Pericardial thickening on the images","Unexplained wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more","Ejection fraction under 35% with dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is the typical diagnostic test of choice, and the clinical diagnosis is confirmed when unexplained increased left ventricular wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more is found anywhere in the wall. The hypertrophy is most often asymmetric, with septal hypertrophy as the classic pattern and a small left ventricular cavity. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What does a normal electrocardiogram suggest when dilated cardiomyopathy is suspected?","choices":["It has no value here","It makes it unlikely","It confirms early disease","It points to alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram is usually abnormal in dilated cardiomyopathy, so a normal tracing will almost rule it out. Typical abnormalities include left ventricular hypertrophy, atrial fibrillation, premature ventricular contractions and conduction delays such as left bundle branch block. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
